[Music]  if you're a fan of role-playing games  you've probably played one Chris Avalon  has worked on there's something unique  about each of them whether it's the deep  and mature a narrative of clean scape  torment the way Knights of the Old  Republic to deconstructs the light side  and dark side morality of Star Wars or  the fact that South Park the stick of  truth was actually a really good South  Park game now Avalon is working with in  exile entertainment on wasteland 2 it's  the sequel to the original  post-apocalyptic RPG which later  inspired the Fallout series a three  million dollar Kickstarter campaign  means the team can bring back wasteland  one's old-school role-playing with a  modern presentation but what does it  actually mean to write and design for  the sequel to a 16 year old game  according to Avalon it's all about  making the story more reactive I think  what makes wasteland 2 different than  other RPGs I've worked on is a sheer  amount of reactivity chains because that  was such a big part at wasteland one a  lot of the reactivity chains for  wasteland to have at least three steps  when other RPGs I think there might have  been one period of reactivity to it or  like one choke point where someone  reacts to it but but in wasteland 2 they  made a really conscious effort to keep  those chains going for a very very long  time sort of which you don't see for  many many hours into the game which i  think is is great and that point you  pretty much committed to it and married  to it which i think makes for much more  enriching reactive experience personally  so for example you could meet some  bartender called Gorgon ovitch in the  rail nomads you can buy snake squeezes  from him you can learn that they're  pretty crappy quality we don't tell the  player hey go back and talk to this guy  get pissed at him but we assume that  players would probably some players  probably want to they're like you know  what I spent money on this this guy  [ __ ] robbed me I'm gonna go back and  confront him on this even though no  one's expressly telling me to do it and  then once that happens because it makes  logical sense that she would do that  only as a player like I mean you know I  thought I was playing a D&amp;D game I'd go  find that guy beat him up and then  having a quest sparked off of that  usually it's things that players would  logically think to do that we actually  keep the reactivity change going in some  instances and other times though  supposedly referenced the quest giver or  the specific event and  the plug is all because I did that I  understand why this is occurring you're  free to go around and beat people up  because wasteland 2 doesn't discriminate  against how you want to build your  character if you're allowing the player  to build their character a certain way  you have to respect their choices and  make sure those choices are viable  because if you give the player a bunch  of options to build a character that  ends up being overall terrible they're  having a should credibly crappy time  playing the game well then that's on the  designers side not on the players side  so what you want to do is if you're  giving the player the option to build  their character say whether there was  like a smartass a kiss-ass a hard-ass a  combat guy a stealth guy you want to  make sure there's cool things for each  of those types of characters to do and  moments were they shine or they're like  you know what because I was a smartass  in this situation I feel really good  really good that I how witted that NPC  or found that solution they're probably  very few other people would have done  because they didn't specialize in the  skill that I did and if outsmarting  people isn't your thing don't worry the  harsh environment of wasteland to  facilitate some violence in a way that  few other RPGs do sometimes what games  will do is they want to preserve all  quest givers and like make them  invulnerable so if you actually do  trying to attack them like there's no  way to kill them for example which feels  really weird especially if you do want  to be a violent character it's a very  violent world and one of the design  mandates for wasteland 2 was that you  actually you know you actually just  can't kill everybody if you want to like  so no one's really immortal or  untouchable killing everybody will have  its consequences but not in the form of  good or evil points this avoids a key  problem that Avalon experienced on  Knights of the Old Republic - it was  very difficult in dialogue situations to  try and get the player away from making  just the dark side on my side choice I  mean like in some instances they  wouldn't even care that much about the  context they would just go ok what's the  light side response here and in that  instance that feels like it's doing the  narrative disservice because the player  is not even thinking about the situation  they're involved with they're just  thinking about how do I get more light  side or dark side points wasteland  avoids that issue by going you know what  we don't have a morality system like  that you can approach each situation as  an individual and make an individual  choice for that situation  you just it's it that that is just you  role-playing that is the sitting you  want to make and there might be  consequences of doing that but and  you're able to sort of take that  situation and not worry about bad karma  good karma or you know light side dark  side it's just like you can focus in the  situations just think well what should I  do here or what do I feel like doing  they just accept the consequences  they're on so all that's left to ask  yourself is how are you going to play  wasteland 2 or perhaps more importantly  are you prepared to deal with the  consequences  [Music]

